.TH PSIM 1 "2026-11-18" "psim 1.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
psim \- readable process table from ps output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B psim
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ \-\- N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B psim
obtains the process list from
.BR ps (1)
and prints it as an aligned table with optional colours, a totals footer,
sorting, filtering and a process tree.
.PP
On a terminal,
.B psim
runs as a refreshing monitor with keyboard and mouse shortcuts.
When standard output or standard input is not a terminal, or when
.B \-\-N
or
.BI \-\-watch " 0"
is given, it prints a single static frame: one line per process, without ANSI
sequences, padding or truncation, so that it composes with pipes and files like
.BR ps .
.PP
By default the table uses the simplified column set
.RB ( \-\-newbie );
.B \-\-normal
selects the full column set.
Terminals narrower than 100 columns are forced to the simplified set, and the
interactive mode refuses terminals smaller than 60x6.
.PP
Short options may be clustered:
.B psim \-pn
is
.BR "psim \-p \-n" ,
.B psim \-w5
passes 5 to
.BR \-w ,
and
.B psim \-gfirefox
passes
.I firefox
to
.BR \-g .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-pid
Sort by PID (ascending).
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-ppid
Sort by parent PID (ascending).
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-cpu
Sort by CPU usage, highest first.
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-mem
Sort by resident memory, largest first.
.TP
.B \-N, \-\-nice
Sort by nice value (ascending).
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-user
Sort by owner name (ascending).
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-cmd
Sort by command name (ascending) and show the command line.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-tree
Arrange processes under their parent according to the parent PID.
The active sort orders siblings.
A process whose parent is not in the list starts a new root, so the result is
a forest rather than a truncated branch.
Indentation is applied to the command and argument columns only; PID columns do
not move.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tree
Cancel
.BR \-\-tree ,
including a
.B TREE=1
inherited from the configuration file.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-newbie
Simplified display: PID, USER, resident memory in MiB, %CPU and the command.
This is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-normal
Full display: PID, PPID, USER, NI, RSS, %MEM, %CPU and the command.
Cancels
.BR \-\-newbie .
.TP
.B \-k, \-\-kernel
Include kernel threads.
.TP
.B \-K, \-\-kernel-only
Show kernel threads only.
.B \-k
and
.B \-K
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.BI \-g " PATTERN" "\fR, \fP\-\-filter " PATTERN
Keep only processes whose owner, command name or arguments match the extended
regular expression
.IR PATTERN ,
as understood by the installed
.BR awk .
An invalid expression is rejected before the session starts.
In the interactive mode the same filter can be edited live with
.BR / .
.TP
.BI \-w " N" "\fR, \fP\-\-watch " N
Refresh every
.I N
seconds.
Zero means a single static frame.
.I N
ranges from 0 to 3599; values at or above 3599 freeze the display immediately.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-no-header
Hide the header line.
Static output never prints a header.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-no-footer
Hide the footer line.
Static output never prints a footer.
.TP
.B \-O, \-\-fullcmd
Show the full command line, with arguments, instead of the command name alone.
.TP
.B \-0, \-\-no-color
Disable colours.
Colours are also disabled automatically when the output is not a terminal or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb .
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-machine-cpu
Express %CPU rows and totals as a percentage of the whole machine instead of
one core.
.TP
.B \-\-per\-core
Express %CPU rows and totals per core, where 100% is one core.
This is the default and cancels
.BR \-M .
.TP
.B \-\-no-config
Do not read the configuration file at all.
The session starts from the built\-in defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Do not hide errors from
.B ps
and
.BR awk .
.TP
.BI \-\- N
Print the first
.I N
processes once, then exit.
Zero means all processes.
.I N
is limited to 10000.
This is a static mode.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the complete built\-in help and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing.
.SH OUTPUT
The simplified table contains PID, USER, resident memory (MiB), %CPU and the
command.
The full table adds PPID, NI, RSS and %MEM.
With
.BR \-O ,
the whole command line is shown.
.PP
%CPU is the value reported by
.BR ps (1)
relative to one core, unless
.B \-M
is active.
.PP
The footer,
.BR "TOTAL (displayed)" ,
sums the resident memory of the displayed rows, their %MEM (full table only)
and their %CPU in the same unit as the rows, followed by the displayed and total
process counts.
.PP
In interactive mode, a status line to the right of the header reports the
active filter, freeze state and scroll position.
When the header is hidden, the status is printed on the last line instead.
.SH "INTERACTIVE MODE"
Interactive mode is entered when both standard input and standard output are
terminals and neither
.B \-\-N
nor
.B \-\-watch 0
was given.
It requires at least 60 columns and 6 lines.
.PP
The terminal is restored on exit, including on interrupt or termination.
.SS Keys
.TP
.B /
Edit the filter.
Each keystroke filters the list live; Enter applies, Escape cancels,
Backspace deletes a character and Ctrl\-U clears the input.
.TP
.B c
Toggle sorting by CPU.
.TP
.B m
Toggle sorting by resident memory.
.TP
.B p
Toggle sorting by PID.
.TP
.B P
Toggle sorting by parent PID.
.TP
.B N
Toggle sorting by nice value.
.TP
.B u
Toggle sorting by owner.
.TP
.B C
Toggle sorting by command; also shows the command line.
.TP
.B t
Toggle the process tree.
.TP
.B k
Cycle kernel threads: hidden, included, kernel threads only.
.TP
.B M
Toggle %CPU between per core and whole machine.
.TP
.B h
Toggle the header.
.TP
.B f
Toggle the footer.
.TP
.B o
Toggle the command line column.
.TP
.B +
Increase the refresh interval by one second, up to 120.
.TP
.B \-
Decrease the refresh interval by one second, down to 1.
.TP
.B F
Freeze or resume refreshing.
.TP
.B "Left arrow"
Switch to the simplified display.
.TP
.B "Right arrow"
Switch to the full display.
.TP
.B "Up arrow, Down arrow"
Move the selection by one row.
Holding the key accelerates the movement.
.TP
.B Escape
Release the selection.
.TP
.B "PgUp, PgDn"
Scroll the view by one page.
.TP
.B "Home, End"
Jump to the first or last page.
.TP
.B "Enter, d"
Show details for the selected process.
.TP
.B x
Send SIGTERM to the selected process, after confirmation.
.TP
.B X
Send SIGKILL to the selected process, after confirmation.
.TP
.B ?
Show the complete key map as an overlay.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SS Mouse
A left click selects the row under the pointer.
A double click on the same row opens the process details.
Shift+click keeps the terminal's own text selection available.
.SS "Process termination"
Signals are sent only after an explicit confirmation.
PID 1,
.B psim
itself and its own ancestors are always refused.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B psim
reads
.I ~/.config/psim/default.conf
(see
.B FILES
for the exact location) at startup.
The file contains one
.I KEY=VALUE
pair per line; a
.B #
starts a comment.
It is never evaluated as shell code.
Unknown keys and invalid values are reported on standard error and ignored.
Command\-line options override the configuration.
.PP
Recognised keys:
.TP
.B NEWBIE
1 for the simplified table, 0 for the full table.
.TP
.B INTERVAL
Refresh interval in seconds, 0 to 3599; 0 means static.
.TP
.B KERNEL
0 (hidden), 1 (included) or 2 (kernel threads only).
.TP
.B SORT
One of
.BR pid ,
.BR \-pid ,
.BR ppid ,
.BR user ,
.BR cmd ,
.BR rss ,
.BR %cpu ,
.BR %mem ,
.BR nice ,
or the same names prefixed with a minus sign for descending order.
.TP
.B FILTER
Extended regular expression used at startup.
.TP
.B SHOW_OPTS
1 to show the full command line.
.TP
.B HEADER
1 to show the header.
.TP
.B FOOTER
1 to show the footer.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
1 to disable colours.
.TP
.B LOG
1 to append a session summary to the log (interactive mode only).
.TP
.B DEBUG
1 to keep errors from
.B ps
and
.BR awk .
.TP
.B CPU_MODE
1 for whole\-machine %CPU.
.TP
.B TREE
1 to start in tree mode.
.TP
.B STATS
Obsolete key, accepted without effect.
.PP
Example:
.nf
.ft CR
NEWBIE=0
INTERVAL=2
SORT=-rss
FILTER=firefox|telegram
TREE=1
.ft R
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/psim/default.conf
Configuration file; defaults to
.IR ~/.config/psim/default.conf .
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/psim/log
Session log; defaults to
.IR ~/.cache/psim/log .
It is rotated to
.I log.1
beyond 256 KiB.
The log is written in interactive mode only; static runs never create it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
A
.B dumb
terminal disables colours and decorations.
.TP
.B COLUMNS, LINES
Used, when not attached to a terminal, to lay out the static table.
Defaults to 120x40.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_CACHE_HOME
Relocate the configuration and cache directories.
.TP
.B PSIM_MOUSE_DEBUG
When set and non\-empty, append decoded mouse events to
.I ~/.cache/psim/mouse.log
for debugging.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Normal termination.
.TP
.B 1
Missing
.B ps
or
.BR awk ,
no location for the configuration, or terminal too small for interactive mode.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown option, missing or invalid value, extra argument,
incompatible options, or invalid filter expression.
.TP
.B 130, 131, 143
Interrupted by SIGINT, SIGQUIT/SIGPIPE, or SIGTERM/SIGHUP respectively.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B psim
Monitor all processes in real time.
.TP
.B psim \-g 'firefox|telegram'
Monitor matching processes only.
.TP
.B psim \-\-20
Print the 20 busiest processes once, then exit.
.TP
.B psim \-K \-\-0
Print all kernel threads and exit.
.TP
.B psim \-t \-c
Show the process tree, siblings ordered by CPU.
.TP
.B psim \-k | grep kworker
Filter the static output in a pipe.
.SH NOTES
The interactive footer uses one Nerd Font glyph (U+F449) for the refresh
interval; without a Nerd Font, a placeholder box may appear.
.PP
Tree mode never drops a process: roots whose parent is filtered out are shown
at the top level.
.PP
The choice of
.B awk
matters only for regular expression syntax; gawk, mawk and busybox awk are
supported.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ps (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR awk (1)
